  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure centered south of New England will provide warm\nand mostly dry weather today. Highs will range from near 60F \nnorth of the mountains to near 70F south of the mountains with \nSW winds gusting 15 to 20 mph at times. A trough digging across \nAtlantic Canada will send a frontal zone southward tonight with \nthis front stalling near northern Maine through Tuesday night.\nThis front will provide a focus for clouds and occasional\nshowers with shower chances mainly confined to the mountains\ntowards central Maine. Temperatures will continue to run above\nnormal with lows in the 40s and highs Tuesday ranging from the\nupper 50s near the Canadian Border to low 70s across southern\nNH. Onshore winds will likely keep Tuesday's highs lower than\nMonday across southern and coastal Maine. Total QPF from Monday\nnight through Tuesday night will generally be a tenth of an inch\nor less.\n\nThe stalled front will lift north of the area as a warm front \nWednesday. This will lead to diminishing PoPs across the north \nand skies turning partly to mostly sunny temperatures climb well\nabove normal with highs ranging from the 60s across the north to\nlow 70s south of the mountains. Winds turning onshore will keep\ncoastal and central Maine in the 60s. Above normal temperatures\nlikely continue through at least Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front approaching from the west likely stalls near New \nEngland Thursday night and Friday. This front will help to focus\nrainfall as a couple waves of weak low pressure and moisture \nmove along the front. The front itself just brings the chance \nfor some scattered showers Thursday night into early Friday. But\nwhere it stalls will determine where the heaviest rainfall ends\nup setting up.\n\nThere isn't a strong consensus yet amongst the models, with the\nfocus of the heaviest rain still shifting from run to run and \nmodel suite to model suite. There's still the chance that it \nends up offshore or across southern New England, but also the \nchance that it sets up across northern New England. There is \nalso the chance of tapping into some tropical moisture, with \nthis being more likely in a slower front scenario.\n\nSo while it's too early to draw any strong conclusions, the \npotential exists for some significant rainfall late this week, \nstarting on Friday. Depending on how far west the front stalls, \nrainfall could also continue into the weekend as well.",
